XXXXX (line 6)
DeckReader Error Throwing
DeckReader will throw an IllegalStateException if:
- It cannot find the file at the given path.
- It cannot read the file for whatever reason (I/O).
DeckReader will throw an IllegalArgumentException if:
- Too many/few arguments are given for line 1.
- Arguments 2 and 3 on line 1 are not integers.
- Any line from 2 - 6 is not 5 characters long.
- Any character on line 2-6 is not 'X', 'I', or 'C'.
- 'C' is not found in the middle - line 4, character 3.
- 'C' is found somewhere other than the middle.
- Syntax is otherwise correct, but fails to create a valid Card in the model.


Instantiating user-players
User-Players would likely be an interface similar to a controller, with methods like Place() and
Pass(), alongside methods that allow the providerPlayer to see the view, also with some event listeners in
the Computer Controlled Turn that force it to take a turn (for example). So, the structure
would probably something similar to this:

 - Turn (Interface)
     - UserPlayer (Class - represents a person playing)
     - CPUPlayer (Class - represents a computer playing)

The Computer Controlled Turn would likely be designed so that it has a list of priorities, which
check in a row and perform some function if a condition is met. It would probably look something
like this:
- Try to place the highest value providerCard the furthest forward on the board. If it is one cheaper than
        the current spot, try to find another eligible one.
- If no spots are eligible, try the next highest value providerCard in the hand.
- If nothing can be placed, pass the turn.

///// PART 2 //////

Big Differences from the previous assignment:
- Decoupled QueensBlood, Card (now named Unit), and QueensBloodTextualView by adding the interfaces PawnsBoardModel, Card, QueensBloodView which they inherit.
    - This allows them to exist with other class implementations in preparation for the final assignment
    - This used to force functionality to be implementation specific, but now allows for multiple concrete implementations under the same interface umbrella
- QueensBloodTextualView now inherits the same type as the QueensBloodGUIView, which means it needs an appendable in its constructor
    - QueensBloodTextualView is still a view, and it should function as one. This just made sense.
- Added new methods to PawnsBoardModel/ReadonlyPawnsBoardModel
    - Some were simply mandated by the assignment, others are used for testing the strategies.
- Added a method in Turn that simply gets the other providerPlayer (i.e. PLAYER1.getOther() == PLAYER2)
    - This reduces duplicate code and looks better.
- Tests were also decoupled, now using the interfaces instead of being dependent on the concrete classes.

Key Commands for the View:
- Pass turn with backspace, and confirm view with enter.
NOTE: This has been changed! The controls are now:
- Pass turn with P, confirm with M.

Extra Credit:
- We didn't have time for extra credit, you don't have to look.

Note:
- The move implementation probably merits some explanation - it functions as a macro, or a representation of a command which can be ran with the method execute(model), which calls the current object on the given model.

//// PART 3 /////

Big Differences from the previous assignment:
- The view can only use one final Turn object, specified upon construction instead of dynamically displaying whatever the model's current turn is
    - This means that only one hand is displayed at a time in the view
- PawnsBoard now has a method, addNotified(), which allows it to take in objects it notifies when the turn changes
- QueensBloodController new extends the Notifiable interface, as to notify controllers upon the changing of turns
- checkPosition() in QueensBlood previously didn't check much of what it was supposed to, for some reason. This is now fixed.

Command-Line Arguments - Only 4 are allowed. Any other amount causes an error to be thrown.
- First argument: File path to Red's deck
- Second argument: File path to Blue's deck
- Third argument: Describes a providerPlayer/strategy for the red providerPlayer (one of "human", "fillfirst", "maxrowscore")
- Fourth argument: Describes a providerPlayer/strategy for the red providerPlayer (one of "human", "fillfirst", "maxrowscore")
If an invalid path is given for the first or second argument, the default docs/deck.config is used and no error is thrown.
If an invalid strategy is given for the third or fourth argument, a human controller/providerPlayer is used and no error is thrown.

//// PART 4 ////

Big Differences:
- To fix some problems using Backspace as our pass key on Macs, the controls have been changed:
    - P is now the key for pass
    - M is now the key for confirm
- Passing has now been fixed for Mac users.
- Ending the game with a tie no longer crashes it (this was caused by the game returning a null winner, which the end of game text didn't account for).
- Running the game with two CPU Players now no longer endlessly shows dialogues, instead showing only two then the end game state in each view.


Changes made to Provider Code:
- Style fixes.
- Given the similarities in naming the provider's code and our code, we have chosen to rename the given provider code classes
  to avoid naming similarities and errors down the line. Classes whose names were similar to provider code were renamed by adding
  'Provider' as a prefix.
    - Ex. Card -> ProviderCard
  - Classes changed this way include:
    - Provider Controller:
        - ProviderMove
    - Provider Model:
        - ProviderCard
        - ProviderCell
        - ProviderIPawnsBoard
        - ProviderPlayer
        - ProviderReadonlyPawnsBoard
No implementation or interface details in the Provider Code has been changed otherwise.


Features we were able to get working on their view:
- Showing the current game state on the board, including pawns, cards and the score on the left and right
- The controller can select and play elements on the board, and send messages to the player
- The game can end, with a message saying it does so
- The game can handle errors, with a message telling the player what went wrong
Features we were unable to get working:
- Their view, for some reason, cannot display the selected card or hand. I believe this has something to do with how their model handles
selected cards instead of their controller/view (like our setup), but I was unable to get this working despite numerous attempts and seeing
that their view properly reads from our model when selecting a tile.
















